      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Since $x^{\\log_x 4} = 4$ and $x^{\\log_{27} 3} = 3$, thus we have \\[\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log x}{\\log 27} = \\frac{\\log x}{\\log 3^3}\\] $\\Rightarrow (\\log x)^4 = 16\\log 3$, which gives $(2\\log x)^2 = (\\log 3)^3$.\n\nLet $\\log 3 = 1$, thus $2\\log x = 1$ leads to $\\log x = \\frac 12$, from which $x = 2^{\\frac 12} = \\sqrt 2$.\n\nSo $\\boxed{\\sqrt 2}$ is one of the solutions to the given equation. Alternatively, we could interpret (*) as $x^{\\log_{27} 4} = 27$, from which $x = (27)^{\\frac 14} = 3^{\\frac 13}$.",
