    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We start from the given identity $\\log_x 4=\\log_{27} 3$. We convert both logarithmic expressions to the same base of the numbers.\n\nUsing the natural logarithm, we have $\\ln 4=\\ln 3$, given that $\\ln e=1$, and since $4=e^{\\ln 4}$ and $3=e^{\\ln 3}$.\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(\\ln 4)^{x^{-1}}&=(\\ln 3)^{27^{-1}} \\\\\n\\ln 4^x&=\\ln 3^{27^{-1}} \\\\\n(\\ln 4)^x&=\\ln 3^{27^{-1}}\\ln e \\\\\n4^x&=\\left(e^{\\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 4}}\\right)^{27^{-1}}\\ln e \\\\\n4^x&=e^{27^{-1}(\\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 4})}\\ln 4 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nSuppose we take the natural logarithm of both sides of the equation:\n\\[\\ln(4^{x})=\\ln(e^{27^{-1}(\\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 4})}\\ln 4)\\]\n\nCombining the exponential terms, we have:\n\\[x\\ln(4)=\\frac{\\ln(e\\ln 4)}{\\ln(3)}\\ln 4 + 27^{-1}\\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 4}\\]\n\nUsing the natural logarithm of $e$, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\nx\\ln(4)&=\\frac{\\ln(4)}{\\ln 3}\\ln 4 + 27^{-1}\\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 4} \\\\\nx\\ln(4)&=\\ln(4)^{1} + \\frac{27^{-1}27}{\\ln(4)}\\ln 3 \\\\\nx\\ln(4)&=\\ln(4)^{1} + \\ln(\\sqrt{3})\\ln 4 \\\\\nx&=\\frac{\\ln(4)}{\\ln 4} + \\frac{\\ln(\\sqrt{3})\\ln 4}{\\ln(4)} \\\\\nx&=1 + \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 3\\ln 4}{\\ln(4)} \\\\\nx&=1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 3 \\\\\nx&=1 + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\boxed{x = 1 + \\frac{1}{2}}$.",
